{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And these are the ordinances.</b> Wherever \"אֵלֶּה\" is said it invalidates the preceding; \"וְאֵלֶּה\" adds to the preceding — just as the preceding were from Sinai, so these are from Sinai. And why is the passage of ordinances juxtaposed to the passage of the altar? To tell you that you shall seat the Sanhedrin beside the Sanctuary (Mekhilta)." "<b>Which you shall set before them.</b> The Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses: Do not let it enter your mind to say, I shall review the chapter and the ruling with them two or three times until it is ordered in their mouths like its mishnah, but I shall not trouble myself to make them understand the reasons of the matter and its explanation — therefore it is said אֲשֶׁר תָּשִׂים לִפְנֵיהֶם — like a table set and ready to eat before a person (ibid.)." "<b>Before them.</b> And not before gentiles; and even if you know of one law that they judge according to the laws of Israel, do not bring it before their courts — for one who brings the laws of Israel before gentiles profanes the Name and magnifies the name of idolatry to praise it, as it says ״כִּי לֹא כְצוּרֵנוּ צוּרָם וְאֹיְבֵינוּ פְּלִילִים״ (Deuteronomy 32) — when our enemies act as judges, that is testimony to the exaltedness of their fear of Heaven (Tanchuma)."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:53:26.183569062Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}